{
  "blocks": [
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "String questions come in many costumes — palindromes, anagrams, brackets, windows, matching, editing — but underneath, most of them are one of nine moves. Learning to **recognise the move** from the wording is what makes a new question feel familiar."
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "text": "Three questions to ask"
    },
    {
      "type": "list",
      "ordered": true,
      "items": [
        "**What's the alphabet?** *\"Lowercase English letters\"* means you can count with an array of 26. *\"Any characters\"* means a hash map.",
        "**Can I change the input?** If the string is handed over as a character array (or you're in C++), you may work in place. Otherwise, you'll build a new string — with a builder.",
        "**What's being asked?** A yes/no about the whole string, the best **substring**, all **combinations**, the position of a **pattern**, or a minimum/maximum between **two strings** — each points to a pattern below."
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "text": "The 9 patterns, from easiest to hardest"
    },
    {
      "type": "drawing",
      "src": "/drawings/db062ef8-331a-4b0e-8889-1c06f68343d7.svg",
      "alt": "Nine cards in three columns, ordered from easiest (green) to hardest (red): two pointers, frequency count, scan and build, stack, sliding window, expand the centre, backtracking, string matching and dynamic programming on strings, each with a short signal.",
      "width": 842,
      "height": 441,
