    {
      "type": "list",
      "ordered": true,
      "items": [
        "**Two pointers** — *\"reverse\"*, *\"palindrome\"*, *\"in place\"*. One pointer at each end, moving inwards, comparing or swapping. Reverse String, Valid Palindrome, Valid Palindrome II, Reverse Words in a String.",
        "**Frequency counting** — *\"anagram\"*, *\"same characters\"*, *\"first unique\"*, *\"can you build X from Y\"*. Count each character (array of 26 or a hash map) and compare the counts. Valid Anagram, First Unique Character, Group Anagrams.",
        "**Scan and build** — *\"compress\"*, *\"convert\"*, *\"parse\"*, *\"common prefix\"*. Walk the string once, keep a little state, and write the answer as you go. Longest Common Prefix, String Compression, String to Integer (atoi).",
        "**Stack** — *\"backspace\"*, *\"remove adjacent\"*, *\"valid brackets\"*, *\"undo\"*. The newest character is the one that can be cancelled. Backspace String Compare, Remove All Adjacent Duplicates II, Minimum Remove to Make Valid Parentheses.",
        "**Sliding window** — *\"longest / shortest substring with …\"*, *\"contains all of\"*, *\"anagram of p inside s\"*. Grow the right edge, shrink the left edge, keep counts of what's inside. Find All Anagrams, Longest Repeating Character Replacement, Minimum Window Substring.",
        "**Expand around the centre** — *\"palindromic substring\"*. Every palindrome has a middle; grow outwards from each of the 2n - 1 possible middles. Palindromic Substrings, Longest Palindromic Substring.",
        "**Backtracking** — *\"all possible\"*, *\"generate every\"*, *\"combinations\"*. Build the string one choice at a time, and undo the choice to try the next. Letter Combinations of a Phone Number, Generate Parentheses.",
        "**String matching** — *\"find the pattern\"*, *\"is it made of repeats\"*, *\"longest prefix that is also a suffix\"*. The KMP prefix function finds a pattern in O(n + m) instead of O(n · m). First Occurrence, Repeated Substring Pattern, Shortest Palindrome.",
        "**Dynamic programming on strings** — *\"minimum edits\"*, *\"longest common subsequence\"*, *\"can it be split into words\"*. A table over prefixes of one or two strings, each cell built from its neighbours. Longest Common Subsequence, Word Break, Edit Distance."
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "text": "Substring or subsequence?"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "This one word changes the pattern, so read it carefully. A **substring** is a contiguous block (`\"ell\"` in `\"hello\"`) — think two pointers, sliding window or expand-around-centre. A **subsequence** keeps the order but may skip characters (`\"hlo\"` in `\"hello\"`) — that usually means dynamic programming."
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "text": "Choosing by the size of the input"
    },
    {
      "type": "list",
      "ordered": false,
      "items": [
        "**n up to about 20** — trying every possibility is fine: backtracking, O(2ⁿ) or similar.",
        "**n up to a few thousand** — O(n²) works: expand around every centre, a DP table over two strings.",
        "**n up to 10⁵ or more** — you need O(n) or O(n log n): two pointers, one scan, a sliding window, counting, KMP."
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "The rest of the course takes the patterns one at a time, in exactly this order, each through the interview questions that use it most."
    }
